\title{First step of an error bound for the main path of \code{\verb~|expf~}}
\taxon{Session}
\date{2026-11-23}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{exp}
\tag{error-bound}
\meta{harness}{Claude Code}
\meta{model-id}{claude-fable-5-2}
\import{base-macros}

\p{After the reviews that began with [ses-9U0D](ses-9U0D), the maintainer raised that the exhaustive computation does not scale to binary64 and asked for an experiment: bound the error of the path through the tables and the polynomial with the automation that FloatLib has, before importing Gappa or Sollya. Branch \code{\verb~|explore/expf-error-bound~}, from the main branch.}
\ul{
\li{\strong{Result.} The approximation part is proved: [log-7DAL](log-7DAL). FloatLib's \code{\verb~|interval~} was enough, with one reformulation: [ins-ZOZE](ins-ZOZE). No external tool is in the proof.}
\li{\strong{Advisers.} Sollya and Gappa were run from the flake to know what to expect. Their numbers are in [ins-ZOZE](ins-ZOZE) and agree with what Lean proved for the approximation.}
\li{\strong{Not done.} The evaluation in binary64, the tables, the reduction of the argument and the conclusion for an input: [prob-48KJ](prob-48KJ) lists them in order with what was found for each.}
}
\p{\strong{Surveyed in FloatLib, read but not used:} \code{\verb~|Numerics/ErrorBound~} propagates absolute errors through operations; \code{\verb~|Numerics/Enclosure/Expression~} is the checker behind \code{\verb~|interval~}, with a proof of soundness; \code{\verb~|Floats/Formats/BinaryInterchange/Analysis~} has the bound of one operation and the standard model.}
